Solution
This is a typical KodeKloud Jenkins lab. The easiest approach is to create a freestyle job that SSHs to stapp01, creates the MySQL dump, then copies it to ststor01, and schedule it every 10 minutes.

1. Create the Job
Open Jenkins
Login
Username: admin
Password: Adm!n321
New Item

Name:

database-backup
Select Freestyle project
Click OK
2. Build Triggers

Enable:

Build periodically

Cron expression:

*/10 * * * *

Use exactly that expression.

3. Build Step

If the Execute shell option is available, use Build → Execute shell and paste:

#!/bin/bash

TODAY=$(date +%F)
FILE=db_${TODAY}.sql

ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp01 "
mysqldump -u kodekloud_roy -pasdfgdsd kodekloud_db01 > /tmp/${FILE}
"

scp -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp01:/tmp/${FILE} natasha@ststor01:/home/natasha/db_backups/

ssh -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no tony@stapp01 "rm -f /tmp/${FILE}"
4. If Execute Shell is Missing

Install SSH Agent or Publish over SSH plugin.

Most KodeKloud labs already have one of these installed.

If using Publish over SSH:

Configure SSH hosts under:
Manage Jenkins
→ System
→ Publish over SSH

Configure:

stapp01
ststor01

Then add an Execute shell step (or SSH build step depending on the installed plugin) that performs the dump and copy.

